# ⚠️ WHAT PRODUCTION ACTUALLY RAN. One line per migration file, checked by
# `scripts/migration-md5.mjs` on every `npm run test` and by `docs/ci/db-guards.yml`.
#
#     version         md5 of the statement production ran    [md5 of the file now]  [reason]
#
# Two columns means the file is byte for byte the SQL that ran. Four means it has been changed
# since, on purpose, and the third column pins exactly which changed text is allowed — so a
# SECOND edit still fails. The reason is one of the words in REASONS in that script.
#
# ── THE RULE THIS EXISTS FOR ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Never edit a migration production has already applied. Put the fix in a new timestamped
# migration. A migration file is not source code that can be corrected; it is the record of an
# event, and the database it describes has already moved on.
#
# ⚠️ THE RULE HAD BEEN BROKEN THREE TIMES BEFORE ANYTHING COULD SAY SO, which is why the first
# run of this check found eleven files out of step rather than none. Three of them had gained
# re-run guards after the fact (`re-run-guard` below), and only one of those three said so in its
# own header. The other eight predate the body-md5 protocol being worked out at all: seven differ
# only in comments, and `20261027004612` was written back out of the live database with
# `pg_get_functiondef` instead of from what was sent, so it carries that function's own casing and
# is missing one `do $$ … end $$` assertion that did run. All eleven are frozen as they stand —
# the point of the check is the NEXT edit, not an archaeology project on the last one.
#
# ⚠️ AND NOTE WHAT IS NOT PINNED: the file's HEADER. The body rule strips the contiguous `--`
# lines at the top, so a note can always be added above an applied migration without disturbing
# anything here. That is the honest way to annotate one.
#
# ── REGENERATING IT ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There are no database credentials in CI and there must not be, so this is committed rather than
# fetched. After applying a migration, ask production for its record:
#
#     select version || '  ' || md5(statements[1]) as line
#     from supabase_migrations.schema_migrations
#     where version >= '20261027'
#     order by version;
#
# save the lines to a file, then `node scripts/migration-md5.mjs --record <that file>`. Reasons
# already written here are carried over; a divergence nobody has explained is written
# `unexplained`, and the check refuses that word by name rather than letting it pass as a note.
#
# Measured 4 Nov 2026: production holds 362 applied migrations and this directory holds 79 of
# them. Two versions in the record have no file here at all — 20261027002305 and 20261027003416,
# from the day before the practice of committing them started.
#
# ⚠️ A FILE WITH NO LINE HERE FAILS THE CHECK — tightened 4 Nov 2026, the day after this was
# written. It was merely REPORTED at first and the run still exited 0, which left `npm run test`
# stricter than the script itself: the suite asserted every file had a line, while the gate in
# `docs/ci/db-guards.yml` only mentioned it. A migration applied without its hash recorded is the
# very gap this exists to close, so it stops the build now.
#
# The order that keeps that quiet is: apply the migration, commit the file, regenerate below. A
# file committed BEFORE it has been applied has no record to compare and fails too — which is the
# same rule read from the other end, since `apply_migration` is how DDL reaches this project.
#
# The one exception is the schema baseline `00000000000000_baseline.sql`, which is a `db dump`
# rather than a statement anybody ran, so this record will never hold a row for it. It is named in
# DUMPS in that script rather than inferred from anything — one filename, not a pattern.
#
